# General Debate on the Final Supplementary Demands for 1967-68
Tamil title: 1967-68ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான இறுதித் துணை நிதிக் கோரிக்கைகள்மீது பொது விவாதம்

Speech delivered on 27 March 1968.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Chairman, it appears that in earlier times it was thought unnecessary to set out many views in such an elaborate manner during a general debate on the final supplementary estimates, as has been done today. Today, however, many members have expressed their views on these final supplementary estimates at considerable length.

Although the matters do not concern them directly, members—and particularly the Leader of the Opposition—also spoke about several emotionally charged ideas and events occupying their minds today. I hope they will accept that this Government feels no less strongly about any of these matters than they do.

They pointed out that students in hostels are now facing difficulties and hardships and said either that the Government had failed to notice them or that it ought to do so. A few months ago they complained that, whatever happened to students, this Government immediately went to support them; that whatever students said, the Government at once took their side; and that it yielded as soon as students insisted. Our Congress members even said that it was wrong for us to have passed certain resolutions merely because students demanded them.

If I remember correctly, I said that this Government did not hesitate to listen to students concerning a problem that tormented their minds. Both then and now, whether they are students, workers or members of any other section of society, this Government will accept their views when they are just. If any of them suffers distress, it will act to prevent it. If clashes arise among them, it will try to stop them. If events nevertheless go beyond us, it will take proper measures to keep them from assuming terrifying proportions. Afterwards, this Government will do its duty in establishing justice and providing relief. I wish to stress that now.

I therefore believe I am saying only that the Leader of the Opposition gave me certain information, not that he alleged the Government had failed to perform certain duties. When events unfold at lightning speed, it is not easy for any Government to stop them as they pass from one stage to the next. If there is an oil lamp and its light diminishes, one may pour in oil; when it grows dim one may trim the wick. But when lightning flashes, we cannot control its effect. Likewise, when events move with the speed of lightning, it is not reasonable merely to ask why their consequences could not have been reduced beforehand.

The Leader of the Opposition and the members of the House know well the anguish and distress I have undergone during these days of responsibility. To the best of my knowledge, senior police officers have worked during the past two or three days with a strong sense of responsibility, striving to prevent any incident from spreading or growing. Violence has occurred. Why did it occur? What caused it? In what ways could it have been prevented? I trust evidence on all these questions will be sought in the judicial inquiry.

Members asked what the inquiry's “terms of reference” would be. They have not yet been defined. Members need have no doubt that, as far as possible, they will include every aspect.

As for the supplementary estimates, the Leader of the Opposition asked why they had to be brought forward twice and whether this did not show some lack of skill in preparing the Budget. It is not because of any lack of budgeting skill. The need for these estimates is set out in my speech.

Nor is this the first time that final supplementary estimates have been presented for a second time. I think it necessary to state whether this has happened before. Thiru Vinayakam may say, “Let what happened earlier remain in the past; what is the position now?” Such an argument may suit the philosophy that one must find fault simply because one sits on that side, but otherwise it is not sound. They might create an impression in the country by arguing, “Others could manage with one supplementary estimate or supplementary budget, but you could not.” I must speak so that no such impression arises.

Supplementary estimates have previously been submitted many times. In some years they were brought twice, in some three times, and in still others even four times. We, with less experience, have submitted them twice. This does not mean there was some want of care in preparing the Budget.

This criticism is made not only about our State Budget but everywhere in general: “At first they say this much revenue will come, but later more comes. They say this much will be spent, but afterwards they spend more. Is there no certainty in it? Could all this not have been known in advance?”

When I raised this question, the explanation given by Thiru Subramaniam, who was then Finance Minister, satisfied me. I therefore cite it. This should not be taken as though I am merely saying: “Those now in opposition repeat what I said when I was in opposition, so I, now in government, may repeat what they said when they were in government.” I wish to say that their answer satisfied me even then because it was reasonable.

Whenever a Government prepares a Budget, it leaves a little “breathing space,” allowing some room to move this way or that; it does not make every figure absolutely final. If it states definitively, “Rs. 15 crore will be spent under this head,” and is then forbidden to spend anything more, the Government may be unable to carry out useful work when needs arise in the meantime. That is why such flexibility exists.

Detailed explanations have been given showing the basis on which these supplementary estimates were prepared and why the amounts are justified and are not being spent on improper purposes. I have stated that these final supplementary estimates were prepared on the basis of the most recent assessment of expenditure trends under the various heads. The figures reflect the “latest position.” They show expenditure rising somewhat above the revised estimates. That rise is not without cause; there are many reasons.

One important area of additional expenditure is the provision of matching grants corresponding to the sums earned by panchayat unions from house tax, the local cess surcharge and similar revenue sources. An additional provision of Rs. 50 lakh is required. The Government has agreed to provide this matching grant under the rules. No Government would describe it as needless excess expenditure.

Another item is expenditure on maternal and child welfare centres. A provision of Rs. 15 lakh is made for the higher grants payable to them. Nobody can doubt that it is justly due. Indeed, judging from the speeches of some members, one might conclude that still more should have been given to this department if resources had permitted.

Another item provides an additional Rs. 8.5 lakh to repair damage to the roads and buildings of local bodies. Similarly, Rs. 47.5 lakh is provided for loans to cultivators to purchase chemical fertilisers. Under this head generally, provision has now been increased to Rs. 2 crore from the earlier Rs. 1 crore.

For the special minor-irrigation scheme implemented through the Board of Revenue, the earlier provision of Rs. 108 lakh proved insufficient; it has therefore been increased to Rs. 165 lakh. I have placed all these matters before the House. These supplementary estimates are reasonable, necessary and submitted in accordance with established practice. I therefore humbly request the House to accept them.

**The Hon. Deputy Speaker:** I think all the demands may now be put to the vote together.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Chairman, I omitted to ask them to withdraw their cut motions because I trusted that they would accept my request without my having to say so. Though I am sure my confidence will not be in vain, I ask them to withdraw the motions.

---NOTES---
The opening date and title are visibly damaged but recover as 27 March 1968 and the general debate on the final supplementary demands for 1967-68. The first lines of Images 3 and 5 contain layout/OCR loss; their translations follow only the securely recoverable argument and do not attempt to reproduce unreadable fragments. “Breathing space” renders the source's OCR form “உரிதல் ஸ்பேஸ்,” which is likely an English administrative metaphor corrupted in Tamil OCR. The source Tamil remains verbatim.
